<title>SOUTH AFRICA: KUWAIT MAKES STRONG INROADS IN S.AFRICA OIL MARKET.</title>
<headline>KUWAIT MAKES STRONG INROADS IN S.AFRICA OIL MARKET.</headline>
<dateline>CAPE TOWN 1997-08-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Iran remained South Africa's main source of crude oil last year but purchases from Kuwait grew strongly, the South African Petroleum Industry Association said on Monday.</p>
<p>In its latest annual report, Sapia said crude bought by its six members from Iran fell to 68.2 million barrels in 1996 from 80.7 million barrels in 1995 while crude bought from Kuwait rose to 20.9 million barrels from 4.2 million barrels.</p>
<p>The next largest source of supply was Egypt, which supplied 7.7 million barrels, followed by Angola which supplied 6.7 million barrels.</p>
<p>North Sea purchases fell to 3.9 million barrels from 10.2 million barrels while crude bought from Saudi Arabia fell to 2.8 million barrels from 8.2 million barrels.</p>
<p>Total crude purchases stayed steady at about 120.46 million barrels.</p>
<p>Sapia members are BP Southern Africa (Pty) Ltd, Caltex Oil (SA) (Pty) Ltd, Engen Petroleum Ltd , Shell SA (Pty) Ltd, Total South Africa (Pty) Ltd and Zenex Oil (Pty) Ltd.</p>
<p>-- Cape Town newsroom +27 21 25-2238</p>
